<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ninety-five Theses</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1517</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as 95 Theses, Disputation on the Power of Indulgences</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ninety-five Theses are a list of propositions for academic debate written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> in 1517, challenging the sale of indulgences and the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> to remit punishment for sin. Luther sent them to Archbishop Albert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> on 31 October 1517; the story that he nailed them to the door of the Castle Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> comes from later accounts. Printed and spread rapidly across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, they began the controversy that grew into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>.</p></div>
